      "\n",
      "Ladies and gentlemen, the scientific method thrives on the twin pillars of control and comparison. In an era where data generation outpaces our capacity to recruit, train, and maintain human participants, Large Language Models (LLMs) offer an unprecedented solution: a virtual control group that is simultaneously scalable, cost‑effective, and rigorously reproducible. An LLM can be instantiated in thousands of parallel instances, each responding to the same stimulus under identical conditions, thereby eliminating the logistical bottlenecks that plague traditional human cohorts. This scalability ensures that researchers can obtain statistically robust baselines for any experimental paradigm—whether it be linguistic comprehension, decision‑making, or social interaction—without the delays and attrition inherent to human recruitment.\n",
