      "**Opening Speech – Opposition**  \n",
      "\n",
      "Ladies and gentlemen, the proposition’s glittering promise of “scalable, cheap, and perfectly standardised” control groups obscures a fundamental truth: a language model is not a person, and therefore it cannot serve as a legitimate scientific control for human behaviour. Human cognition is rooted in neurobiology, embodied experience, and affective states that no amount of token‑level statistics can emulate. Decision‑making, moral judgement, and social interaction are mediated by hormones, neural circuitry, and lived histories that shape perception in ways that are irreducible to the probability distributions learned from text. When we replace a human cohort with an LLM, we strip away the very substrates—pain, fatigue, anxiety, empathy—that generate the variability we seek to understand. Any “baseline” derived from a model that lacks a body, a brain, and an emotional life is, at best, a caricature, and at worst, a dangerously misleading proxy that will systematically mis‑estimate human performance.\n",
